#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120953} :activity/id #uuid "4f0b1f3d-0571-55a0-be6f-d9254f9a4842" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048396 :topic/id #uuid "9bd96abb-b01a-56d0-979c-1ad6fd88bbd9" :topic/math-academy-id 1845} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120988 :tutorial/content "An **RCL circuit** is an electrical circuit that contains\n\n- a resistor of resistance $R$, measured in ohms,\n\n- a capacitor of capacitance $C$, measured in farads, and\n\n- an inductor of inductance $L$, measured in henries.\n\nThese circuits are commonly studied in the *series* configuration shown below, with a source electromotive force (EMF) $E$, measured in volts.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn an RCL circuit, the current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, is the rate at which charge flows.\n\nTherefore, if the capacitor charge is $q(t)$ (in coulombs), then the current is found by differentiating:\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nq(t)=2e^{-3t}-5e^{-t}+\\dfrac12,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2e^{-3t}-5e^{-t}+\\dfrac12\\right)=-6e^{-3t}+5e^{-t}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26e1009b-25c0-5bda-955d-7918f7667683" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14793 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6520c325-7bbd-5278-b0d0-526cb903b7fa" :step/math-academy-id 603819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119549 :question/id #uuid "c2070894-8653-56e6-af9b-c683b053adc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21893"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051040}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499460}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"} :step/id #uuid "201227a1-db48-5c36-a56f-def337a26a35" :step/math-academy-id 603820 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120986 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIt is possible to write a differential equation that governs the capacitor charge $q(t)$ in a series RCL circuit.\n\nWe know that the voltage drops across the resistor, capacitor, and inductor, respectively, are\n\n$$\nV_R = RI,\n\\qquad\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C},\n\\qquad\nV_L = L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},\n$$\n\nwhere $q(t)$ is the charge on the capacitor and $I(t)$ is the circuit current.\n\nAlso, recall that current is the rate at which charge flows, so\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops:\n\n$$\nE = V_R + V_C + V_L\n$$\n\nSo, substituting the expressions above gives an equation in $q{:}$\n\n$$\nE = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{q}{C} + L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ yields the normalized second-order differential equation governing the charge:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + \\dfrac{R}{L}\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{LC}\\,q = \\dfrac{E}{L}\n$$\n\nTo determine a unique solution $q(t),$ we also need initial conditions, typically\n\n$$\nq(0)=q_0,\n\\qquad\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right|_{t=0}=I(0)=I_0.\n$$\n\nLet's now look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e689d63b-3192-5e5b-8335-f9ca854a99be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14790 :tutorial/title "Finding an Equation Governing the Capacitor Charge"} :step/id #uuid "e38418ad-51f1-5041-a5f6-27f9146042f4" :step/math-academy-id 603823 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120987 :tutorial/content "In the circuit below, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to write a differential equation for the capacitor charge $q(t).$\n\n![](images/b9/b9397930e4753a4c5d1945010cc96641b5a6557e0bf521781ff00f3c90fd328f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 25I = 25\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/40)} = 40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 5\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law ($E=V_R + V_C + V_L$), the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15 = 25\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 40q + 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5,$ we obtain the final differential equation for charge:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = 3\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bb7ca455-c223-53c8-90df-757518f9e830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14792 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "6a229e70-1396-5d2d-b807-48ad2fa1cc31" :step/math-academy-id 603824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120957}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119546 :question/id #uuid "ebb8f782-2616-52d1-8e6a-6c7049f9f9f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21890"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499562}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"} :step/id #uuid "29e6c1d7-d27e-5ad6-a993-f4c5a1d775a3" :step/math-academy-id 603825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120961}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119547 :question/id #uuid "8ac6f533-446a-5d8c-863d-eb220e3dca77" :question/math-academy-id "e-21891"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499560}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"} :step/id #uuid "43dcf4f6-4b0d-5b85-899d-d92751a8a1bf" :step/math-academy-id 603828 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119970 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119548 :question/id #uuid "f5902e80-c98b-599c-bd43-80075e7cc53c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21892"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499558}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"} :step/id #uuid "801f1b52-8834-515f-bbfa-c5a084dc6fc5" :step/math-academy-id 603832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120990 :tutorial/content "For the RCL circuit analyzed previously, the charge $q(t)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = 3.\n$$\n\nRecall that when we solve the differential equation governing the capacitor charge, the solution is often written in the form\n\n$$\nq(t)=q_c(t)+q_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $q_c(t)$ is the **transient charge**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ and\n\n- $q_p(t)$ is the **steady-state charge**, which governs the long-term behavior of $q(t).$\n\nIn a stable RCL circuit with a *constant* electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, and the steady-state charge is the *constant value* the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these conditions into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 5(0) + 8q = 3\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q = 3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state in which the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow. The voltage across the capacitor now exactly balances the source voltage.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is\n$q = \\dfrac{3}{8}\\,\\textrm{C}.$\n\n**Note**: In a stable RCL circuit with a *sinusoidal* electromotive force, the steady-state charge is also sinusoidal." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ab21361-2bac-543c-9a0d-48e35643a23d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14900 :tutorial/title "The Steady-State Capacitor Charge"} :step/id #uuid "27690b14-a860-5a8b-ad9a-d2bcf7d81fcb" :step/math-academy-id 608076 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120958}}] :activity/title "Modeling Capacitor Charge in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120041}]}
